383
00:47:52,800 --> 00:47:53,800
Think of it in those terms.

384
00:47:53,800 --> 00:47:53,800
Think of it in those terms.

385
00:47:53,800 --> 00:47:54,800
Think of it in those terms.

386
00:47:54,800 --> 00:47:59,800
So, how much is the world prepared to pay
right now for a company's future earnings?

387
00:47:59,800 --> 00:48:05,800
Now, if that's the case, the opposite must
also hold true.

388
00:48:05,800 --> 00:48:12,800
How much is the world prepared to sell a
company's forward earnings for?

389
00:48:12,800 --> 00:48:23,800
So, right now, how much is the world
prepared to sell at this moment a
company's future earnings for?

390
00:48:23,800 --> 00:48:30,800
These two statements must hold true at any
one moment in time.

391
00:48:30,800 --> 00:48:31,800
Why?

392
00:48:31,800 --> 00:48:42,800
Because we have a live trading market
where a stock at any one moment in time
has both buyers and sellers.

393
00:48:42,800 --> 00:48:57,800
So, at any one moment in time, the stock
has a price and the stock price is divided
by the future EPS expectation of the
company, of the market.

394
00:48:57,800 --> 00:49:15,800
And the stock price is a representation of
both what the world is willing to pay for
a company's forward earnings and,
simultaneously, what the world is prepared
to sell a company's earnings for.

395
00:49:16,800 --> 00:49:22,800
Now, it's important to get your head
around this concept and make it stick.

396
00:49:22,800 --> 00:49:34,800
The most common measure of price earnings
ratio that is used in the professional
world is a forward-looking PE ratio.

397
00:49:34,800 --> 00:49:37,800
So, a one-year forward PE.

398
00:49:37,800 --> 00:49:56,800
What we're doing with that is we're taking
the market consensus, one-year forward EPS
number, and then we're dividing the
current stock price of today by that
number, by the one-year forward EPS.

399
00:49:56,800 --> 00:50:02,800
And that gives us the forward PE, the
one-year forward PE.

400
00:50:02,800 --> 00:50:20,800
So, for example, if we are sitting in a
moment in time, that's say March 2023, the
one-year forward earnings number would be
the consensus mean number from research
analysts for the year 2023.

401
00:50:20,800 --> 00:50:42,800
In November 2023, in November 2021, as an
example, because we're so much later in
the year, and the year is nearly over, the
one-year forward earnings number that
would be used by professional traders
would be the consensus mean number from
analysts for the year 2022.

402
00:50:42,800 --> 00:50:51,800
So, for the following year, as
professional traders, we do not care about
one-year lagging.

403
00:50:51,800 --> 00:51:04,800
So, the trailing 12-month price earnings
ratio, which is so popularized in
financial markets, we are traders and
we're trading on expectations of the
future.

404
00:51:04,800 --> 00:51:05,800
We look forwards.

405
00:51:05,800 --> 00:51:11,800
So, it's very important to understand this
major concept, guys.

406
00:51:11,800 --> 00:51:29,800
Pro traders view the world and view the
price earnings ratio in a very, very
different way to the way retail traders
view it and even bad pro traders and even
value investors and investors.

407
00:51:29,800 --> 00:51:34,800
So, let's get our head around this
further.

408
00:51:34,800 --> 00:51:52,800
We mentioned, if you think about the way a
PE ratio is calculated, it's the live
stock price of a particular stock divided
by one-year forward-looking consensus mean
earnings.

409
00:51:52,800 --> 00:52:08,800
And stocks trade every day, Monday to
Friday, and analyst EPS estimates
obviously move much less frequently than
the stocks obviously do because they're
trading every day.

410
00:52:08,800 --> 00:52:17,800
Analyst EPS estimates tend to move in
clusters when expectations change.

411
00:52:17,800 --> 00:52:28,800
Stock prices move based on the future
expectations of earnings of the market.

412
00:52:28,800 --> 00:52:33,800
Now, PE is both an absolute and a relative
measure.

413
00:52:33,800 --> 00:52:42,800
If you look at the table on the left-hand
side of the screen, what we've got here is
the oil and gas extraction sector.

414
00:52:42,800 --> 00:52:48,800
And we've got market capitalization and
we've got the forward-looking PE.

415
00:52:48,800 --> 00:52:53,800
And we've filtered in the spreadsheet for
the first year.

416
00:52:53,800 --> 00:52:57,800
So, one-year forward-looking price
earnings ratio.

417
00:52:57,800 --> 00:53:16,800
Now, we've not put on the screen at this
stage the EPS estimates themselves
because, at the moment, this is an
academic exercise so you can understand
this concept of PE interpretation in
isolation.

418
00:53:16,800 --> 00:53:27,800
Now, if you look at the column with
one-year forward-looking PE, so this is
column L, we've highlighted two areas.

419
00:53:27,800 --> 00:53:39,800
We've highlighted oil and gas extraction
stocks that have high PE's and we have oil
and gas extraction stocks highlighted with
low PE's.

420
00:53:39,800 --> 00:53:43,800
So, have a look at those areas on the
screen.

421
00:53:43,800 --> 00:53:48,800
What do you think of the stocks with PE
ratios below 10?

422
00:53:48,800 --> 00:53:51,800
Do you think these stocks are cheap?

423
00:53:51,800 --> 00:53:57,800
What do you think of the stocks with PE
ratios of above 40?

424
00:53:57,800 --> 00:54:00,800
Do you think these stocks are expensive?

425
00:54:00,800 --> 00:54:03,800
Have a think about that for a moment.

426
00:54:03,800 --> 00:54:29,800
Meanwhile, just for the record, stocks
that you have in the table there that have
expected negative EPS, of course, if we
divide a positive stock price and divide
that by a negative EPS, so negative
earnings per share means a company is
losing money, they're going to have a
negative PE ratio.

427
00:54:29,800 --> 00:54:34,800
So, here we're just looking at positive PE
ratios.

428
00:54:34,800 --> 00:54:54,800
And we're looking at oil and gas
extraction stocks with very high PE's, so
PE's over 40, and then oil and gas
extraction stocks with low PE's that are
still positive, but low PE's that are
under 10 times earnings.

429
00:54:54,800 --> 00:54:55,800
So, have you made a decision?

430
00:54:55,800 --> 00:54:58,800
So, have you made a decision?

431
00:54:58,800 --> 00:55:07,800
What do you think of the stocks with PE
ratios below 10 and what do you think of
the stocks with PE ratios above 40?

432
00:55:07,800 --> 00:55:13,800
Are the ones below 10 cheap and are the
ones above 40 expensive?

433
00:55:13,800 --> 00:55:30,800
Well, from a pro trader perspective, I can
tell you right now, two of the biggest
curse words you will ever make as a
professional trader is to say a stock is
either cheap or expensive.

434
00:55:30,800 --> 00:55:37,800
So, let's go through a theoretical example
so you can remember this concept for the
rest of your life.

435
00:55:37,800 --> 00:55:44,800
And the great news about this is that you
only have to learn it once and then it's
done.

436
00:55:44,800 --> 00:55:57,800
All you have to do then is apply this
concept in your initial quantitative
processing phase when seeking out
potential trade ideas.

437
00:55:57,800 --> 00:56:00,800
So, let's run through this example.

438
00:56:00,800 --> 00:56:06,800
And this is a theoretical example of a
stock that is going down.

439
00:56:06,800 --> 00:56:25,800
So, imagine we're sitting in the time of
the year, January, and we're looking at a
stock that has a price of $10 per share
and one year forward earnings estimate of
$1 per share.

440
00:56:25,800 --> 00:56:26,800
What is the PE ratio?

441
00:56:26,800 --> 00:56:27,800
What is the PE ratio?

442
00:56:27,800 --> 00:56:31,800
Well, it's just 10 divided by one.

443
00:56:31,800 --> 00:56:34,800
So, we have a PE of 10.

444
00:56:34,800 --> 00:56:39,800
Now, let's say we fast forward to
February.

445
00:56:39,800 --> 00:56:50,800
So, we're in February the same year, just
one month later, the company announces
that they're not going to hit the market
forecast.

446
00:56:50,800 --> 00:56:56,800
They're not going to hit the $1 per share
in their earnings for the year.

447
00:56:56,800 --> 00:57:03,800
And they're citing some problems in the
supply chain that they're working on
fixing.

448
00:57:03,800 --> 00:57:17,800
And now, their own forecast, the company
forecast, they guide the market to $0.90
per share for their forward earnings for
the year.

449
00:57:17,800 --> 00:57:18,800
Question.

450
00:57:18,800 --> 00:57:27,800
Where does the stock have to go in order
to maintain its 10 times PE ratio?

451
00:57:27,800 --> 00:57:28,800
Where does it have to go?

452
00:57:28,800 --> 00:57:32,800
Yes, you've probably guessed right.

453
00:57:32,800 --> 00:57:40,800
The stock to maintain its 10 times PE
ratio is just $0.90 multiplied by 10.

454
00:57:40,800 --> 00:57:43,800
So, it has to go to $9.

455
00:57:43,800 --> 00:57:46,800
So, a full dollar comes off the share
price.

456
00:57:46,800 --> 00:57:50,800
Now, fast forward a month later.

457
00:57:50,800 --> 00:58:04,800
Let's see what happens a month later in
March when the company announces that
they're no longer going to hit $0.90 a
share for the year in their earnings.

458
00:58:04,800 --> 00:58:10,800
And they're citing a material change in
business conditions.

459
00:58:10,800 --> 00:58:17,800
And they come out and they say, our new
forecast is $0.65 a share for the year.

460
00:58:17,800 --> 00:58:23,800
Where does the stock have to go to in
order to maintain its 10 times PE ratio?

461
00:58:23,800 --> 00:58:25,800
Yes, you've guessed it.

462
00:58:25,800 --> 00:58:28,800
It's just $0.65 multiplied by 10.

463
00:58:28,800 --> 00:58:31,800
So, now the stock is $6.50.

464
00:58:31,800 --> 00:58:36,800
So, we're down $3.50 from $10.

465
00:58:36,800 --> 00:58:37,800
Now, fast forward again.

466
00:58:37,800 --> 00:58:42,800
Let's go forward a few more months to May.

467
00:58:42,800 --> 00:58:51,800
The company announces that they're not
going to hit $0.65 a share for the year in
their earnings.

468
00:58:51,800 --> 00:58:56,800
And now, they're citing a loss of three
key customer contracts.

469
00:58:56,800 --> 00:59:05,800
And their new forecast for the year is
$0.01 per share for this year's earnings.

470
00:59:05,800 --> 00:59:06,800
Question.

471
00:59:06,800 --> 00:59:14,800
Where does the stock have to go to in
order to maintain its 10 times PE ratio?

472
00:59:14,800 --> 00:59:18,800
Yes, it's just $0.01 multiplied by 10.

473
00:59:18,800 --> 00:59:22,800
The stock is now trading at $0.10.

474
00:59:22,800 --> 00:59:30,800
So, earnings per share, which started off
at $1, is down $0.99 to $0.01.

475
00:59:30,800 --> 00:59:32,800
So, 99%.

476
00:59:32,800 --> 00:59:38,800
And the stock price itself is down from
$10 to $0.10.

477
00:59:38,800 --> 00:59:41,800
So, that's down 99%.

478
00:59:41,800 --> 00:59:44,800
So, the point here is very simple.

479
00:59:44,800 --> 00:59:58,800
On the journey that the stock took from
$10 to $0.01 from January till May, it was
on 10 times earnings, 10 times PE ratio,
all the way down.

480
00:59:58,800 --> 01:00:06,800
So, at $10, when the stock was at $10, it
was on 10 times expected future earnings
for the year.

481
01:00:06,800 --> 01:00:19,800
When the stock was at $9, it was on 10
times earnings, because there was $0.90
forecast for the year.

482
01:00:19,800 --> 01:00:23,800
When the stock was at $6.50, it was on 10
times earnings, because $0.65 was forecast
for the year.

483
01:00:23,800 --> 01:00:33,800
And when it was at $0.10, it was on 10
times earnings, because there was $0.01
forecast for the year.

484
01:00:33,800 --> 01:00:36,800
How can we put this more simply?

485
01:00:36,800 --> 01:00:44,800
A stock can be on 10 times earnings, or
the same PE ratio, and keep going down
forever.

486
01:00:44,800 --> 01:00:59,800
Imagine somebody came along to you when
the stock was at $10, and told you that
this stock was cheap, because relative to
other stocks in the sector, the PE ratio
was low.

487
01:00:59,800 --> 01:01:07,800
So, perhaps we had other stocks in the
sector at the time that were on 40 times
PE ratio.

488
01:01:07,800 --> 01:01:24,800
And you listened to them, and you started
believing that the stock at $10 was cheap,
because it's on 10 times earnings, and
it's cheap relative to other stocks in the
sector, and you bought it.

489
01:01:24,800 --> 01:01:30,800
Well, five months later, you would have
been down 99%.

490
01:01:30,800 --> 01:01:43,800
So, a stock can stay cheap forever, and it
can keep going down, and be on the same
multiple all the way down, because their
earnings are going down.

491
01:01:44,800 --> 01:01:50,800
Now, let's have a look at an example where
a stock price goes up.

492
01:01:50,800 --> 01:02:00,800
So, imagine we're sitting in January,
stock has a price of $10 a share, one year
forward earnings estimate, $1 a share.

493
01:02:00,800 --> 01:02:09,800
So, the same situation as before, except
this time, we're looking at an example
where a stock can go up.

494
01:02:09,800 --> 01:02:13,800
And we're looking at PE in this context.

495
01:02:13,800 --> 01:02:24,800
So, let's say in February, that the
company comes out, and they announce that
they're going to beat $1 per share in
their earnings for the year.

496
01:02:24,800 --> 01:02:29,800
And they're citing supply chain
improvements that are increasing
efficiency.

497
01:02:29,800 --> 01:02:36,800
So, now they forecast $1.20 a share for
the year.

498
01:02:36,800 --> 01:02:42,800
Where does the stock have to go in order
to maintain its PE of 10?

499
01:02:42,800 --> 01:02:44,800
Where does it have to go to?

500
01:02:44,800 --> 01:02:45,800
Yes.

501
01:02:45,800 --> 01:02:47,800
Yes, it goes to $12.

502
01:02:47,800 --> 01:03:00,800
Now, in March, the company announces that
they're going to beat $1.20 a share,
citing material changes in business
conditions in the last six weeks.

503
01:03:00,800 --> 01:03:07,800
And now, they're upping their forecast,
their EPS forecast for the year to $2 a
share.

504
01:03:07,800 --> 01:03:14,800
Where does the stock have to go to, to
maintain the PE ratio of 10?

505
01:03:14,800 --> 01:03:17,800
Where does it have to go to?

506
01:03:17,800 --> 01:03:21,800
Yes, it goes to $20.

507
01:03:21,800 --> 01:03:33,800
Now, what happens in May if the company
announces that they're going to beat the
previous $2 per share forecast for the
year in their earnings?

508
01:03:33,800 --> 01:03:41,800
And they're citing a surprise upturn in
the last six weeks in their peripheral
European operations.

509
01:03:41,800 --> 01:03:46,800
And they're now forecasting $4 for EPS for
the year.

510
01:03:46,800 --> 01:03:53,800
So, where does the stock have to go to, to
maintain its 10 times PE ratio?

511
01:03:53,800 --> 01:03:54,800
Where?

512
01:03:54,800 --> 01:03:55,800
Where does it have to go to?

513
01:03:55,800 --> 01:03:58,800
Yes, it goes to $40.

514
01:03:58,800 --> 01:04:15,800
So, imagine back in January, in this
example, when a stock is going up, that
one of your friends told you in January,
when the stock was at $10, that this stock
was expensive, and you should probably go
short the stock.

515
01:04:15,800 --> 01:04:20,800
The stock was expensive relative to other
stocks in the sector.

516
01:04:20,800 --> 01:04:28,800
So, let's say this was a company that had
a high PE relative to other stocks in the
sector.

517
01:04:28,800 --> 01:04:38,800
So, let's say as a 10 PE, that was the
highest PE in the sector, and the rest of
the sector had PE's of five and six.

518
01:04:38,800 --> 01:04:44,800
And your friend tells you that this stock
on a 10 times PE is expensive.

519
01:04:44,800 --> 01:04:46,800
And you shorted the stock.

520
01:04:46,800 --> 01:04:48,800
You listened to your friend.

521
01:04:48,800 --> 01:04:50,800
What would have happened?

522
01:04:50,800 --> 01:04:56,800
Well, you'd be down $30 per share by May.

523
01:04:56,800 --> 01:05:07,800
So, from the long side, when you're
looking at, for example, buying cheap
stocks, and they keep going down forever,
you can lose 100%.

524
01:05:07,800 --> 01:05:21,800
Shorting expensive stocks can be even more
dangerous because theoretically, if EPS is
going up, the stock can keep going up and
maintain its same PE ratio.

525
01:05:21,800 --> 01:05:26,800
At $10, the stock was on 10 times
earnings.

526
01:05:26,800 --> 01:05:30,800
At $12, the stock was on 10 times
earnings.

527
01:05:30,800 --> 01:05:35,800
At $20, the stock was on 10 times
earnings.

528
01:05:35,800 --> 01:05:40,800
And at $40, the stock is on 10 times
earnings.

529
01:05:40,800 --> 01:05:48,800
And your friend, at $40 per share, will
still be telling you that the stock is
expensive.

530
01:05:48,800 --> 01:05:52,800
So, the point here is very, very simple,
guys.

531
01:05:52,800 --> 01:06:09,800
Always, always, always remember, for
stocks that are going down, a stock can go
from any price to its lowest price
possible and still maintain the same PE
ratio.

532
01:06:11,800 --> 01:06:32,800
And by the way, guys, this is not just
limited to the stock market.

533
01:06:33,800 --> 01:06:35,800
This happens everywhere.

534
01:06:35,800 --> 01:06:37,800
This happens in real life.

535
01:06:38,800 --> 01:06:48,800
Let's go through an example as an analogy
of how this occurs everywhere, how this
occurs in real life.

536
01:06:48,800 --> 01:06:55,800
Let's go through this analogy of buying
your wife or girlfriend lingerie for
Valentine's Day.

537
01:06:55,800 --> 01:07:05,800
So, I want you to imagine that it's 6pm on
Valentine's Day and you're leaving the
office or you're leaving your place of
work

538
01:07:05,800 --> 01:07:11,800
You haven't gotten your wife or girlfriend
a Valentine's gift yet.

539
01:07:11,800 --> 01:07:19,800
So, you're about to leave your workplace
and you're planning to go to a department
store on the way home to buy some
lingerie.

540
01:07:20,800 --> 01:07:28,800
And when you actually get to the store and
all of the lingerie, when you get there,
you find out it's sold out.

541
01:07:29,800 --> 01:07:37,800
So, the whole area of lingerie in the
department store is empty, except for
three items.

542
01:07:37,800 --> 01:07:44,800
And you're in a bit of a difficult
situation here because you have to buy one
of them.

543
01:07:44,800 --> 01:07:52,800
Your wife or girlfriend is literally
expecting you home in 30 minutes time for
dinner.

544
01:07:52,800 --> 01:07:58,800
And there's no way you can return home
without a Valentine's gift.

545
01:07:58,800 --> 01:08:05,800
So, there's three items on the hangers in
the lingerie section of the department
store.

546
01:08:05,800 --> 01:08:07,800
And you've got to buy one.

547
01:08:07,800 --> 01:08:10,800
And you're going to have to leave very
quickly.

548
01:08:10,800 --> 01:08:14,800
You're going to have to get home very
quickly with a gift.

549
01:08:14,800 --> 01:08:16,800
Otherwise, there's going to be trouble.

550
01:08:16,800 --> 01:08:20,800
Which one do you choose?

551
01:08:20,800 --> 01:08:23,800
Lingerie A is priced at $50.

552
01:08:23,800 --> 01:08:27,800
Lingerie B is priced at $150.

553
01:08:27,800 --> 01:08:31,800
Lingerie C is priced at $500.

554
01:08:31,800 --> 01:08:35,800
You have to make a decision right now
which one to buy.

555
01:08:35,800 --> 01:08:38,800
Which one do you actually buy?

556
01:08:38,800 --> 01:08:39,800
Think about it.

557
01:08:39,800 --> 01:08:47,800
Now, warning, don't fast forward the video
until you've made a decision.

558
01:08:47,800 --> 01:08:51,800
And remember, if you fail to make a
decision.

559
01:08:51,800 --> 01:09:00,800
So, if you don't make a decision, then it
just means you're going home without a
Valentine's gift for your wife or
girlfriend.

560
01:09:00,800 --> 01:09:02,800
You're going to be in trouble.

561
01:09:02,800 --> 01:09:22,800
So, also, by the way, for female viewers
of the PTM video series, if you're
watching this, please just imagine
yourself on the other side, waiting at
home, waiting for your husband or
boyfriend to come home with your
Valentine's gift.

562
01:09:22,800 --> 01:09:27,800
Imagine yourself as the receiver of the
gift.

563
01:09:27,800 --> 01:09:34,800
What would you want your husband or
boyfriend to bring back with him?

564
01:09:34,800 --> 01:09:37,800
Think about this, guys.

565
01:09:37,800 --> 01:09:40,800
Think about this for a few moments.

566
01:09:40,800 --> 01:09:43,800
I'll give you another 10 seconds.

567
01:09:43,800 --> 01:09:46,800
Lingerie A, $50.

568
01:09:46,800 --> 01:09:49,800
Lingerie B, $150.

569
01:09:49,800 --> 01:09:52,800
Lingerie C, $500.

570
01:09:52,800 --> 01:09:55,800
What have you chosen?

571
01:09:55,800 --> 01:10:03,800
Well, if you went for lingerie A, you have
gone for what you perceive to be the cheap
option.

572
01:10:03,800 --> 01:10:08,800
And, of course, we always have cheap and
expensive in our quotations.

573
01:10:08,800 --> 01:10:15,800
If you've gone for lingerie C, you've gone
for what you perceive to be the expensive
option.

574
01:10:15,800 --> 01:10:25,800
And if you've gone for lingerie B, you've
gone for what you perceive to be the
middle of the road, average priced or
sensible option.

575
01:10:25,800 --> 01:10:35,800
The key here is, guys, is that price in
everything is a signal of quality, no
matter what we buy.

576
01:10:35,800 --> 01:10:45,800
There is a reason why or multiple reasons
why something is priced where it is
priced.

577
01:10:45,800 --> 01:10:57,800
What if I told you that lingerie A is 70%
polyester, 30% cotton, and is the least
desired brand on the market?

578
01:10:57,800 --> 01:11:07,800
What if I told you that the best you can
hope for is that you will get three uses
out of lingerie A, and that's the best
case scenario?

579
01:11:07,800 --> 01:11:17,800
What if I told you that your wife or
girlfriend will probably not even want to
wear it anyway, because it's cheap crap
and it's undesirable.

580
01:11:17,800 --> 01:11:31,800
And in 30 minutes time, when you get home,
she's literally going to slap you around
the face and start crying when you present
it to her as her special Valentine's gift.

581
01:11:31,800 --> 01:11:46,800
You're going to see and perceive lingerie
A completely differently than when you
just made a decision based on price,
because there's a reason why it's $50.

582
01:11:46,800 --> 01:12:01,800
Additionally, what if I told you that the
expensive lingerie C at $500 is 100% pure
silk and is the most desirable brand on
the market?

583
01:12:01,800 --> 01:12:08,800
What if I also told you that you will
easily get 50 uses out of lingerie C?

584
01:12:08,800 --> 01:12:11,800
And also the intangible benefits.

585
01:12:11,800 --> 01:12:24,800
What if I told you that when you get home
and you gift lingerie C to your wife or
girlfriend, she's going to be so
overwhelmed and excited that she's going
to get changed straight into it.

586
01:12:24,800 --> 01:12:37,800
And she's going to wear it with a pair of
high heels while cooking you Valentine's
dinner, give you the night of your life
and you will be to her the best husband or
boyfriend in the world.

587
01:12:37,800 --> 01:12:41,800
Well, for at least the next three months.

588
01:12:41,800 --> 01:12:46,800
Of course, option A no longer seems cheap.

589
01:12:46,800 --> 01:12:49,800
It is now very expensive.

590
01:12:49,800 --> 01:12:53,800
And option C is no longer expensive.

591
01:12:53,800 --> 01:12:55,800
It's now very cheap.

592
01:12:56,800 --> 01:13:01,800
Now, just as a side note, because we are
doing this from the male perspective.

593
01:13:01,800 --> 01:13:13,800
If you're a female watching this and you
were doing it from the perspective of
being at home and waiting for your husband
or boyfriend to come home with the
lingerie.

594
01:13:13,800 --> 01:13:17,800
If you chose lingerie C, no problems
there.

595
01:13:17,800 --> 01:13:18,800
You have high standards.

596
01:13:19,800 --> 01:13:34,800
If you chose lingerie A, I think you need
to be asking yourself some serious
questions in life and probably knocking
your current husband or boyfriend into
shape and maybe think about raising your
standards.

597
01:13:34,800 --> 01:13:41,800
So, jokes aside guys, price is a signal of
quality, okay?

598
01:13:41,800 --> 01:13:45,800
And it's no different in the stock market
with PE ratio.

599
01:13:45,800 --> 01:13:46,800
It's no different.

600
01:13:46,800 --> 01:13:57,800
The multiple that the market pays for a
stock is a signal of the quality of the
earnings, of the quality of the stock.

601
01:13:57,800 --> 01:14:05,800
You will be going into the live stock
market where prices are live every day.

602
01:14:05,800 --> 01:14:15,800
And people, some very smart people in the
world are paying for, open quotes,
expensive stocks at a premium.

603
01:14:15,800 --> 01:14:20,800
And they are selling cheap stocks at a
discount.

604
01:14:20,800 --> 01:14:25,800
And you are going into the market, to the
live market every day.

605
01:14:25,800 --> 01:14:38,800
And if you decide that your strategy is to
buy cheap stocks and sell expensive
stocks, your career is probably going to
be very short lived.

606
01:14:38,800 --> 01:14:41,800
At some point, you will blow up.

607
01:14:41,800 --> 01:14:54,800
At this stage in the quantitative process,
when you're analyzing relative PE ratios,
you always make the following assumptions.

608
01:14:54,800 --> 01:15:00,800
There's nothing you know that the market
doesn't already know.

609
01:15:00,800 --> 01:15:07,800
The market can stay irrational longer than
you can stay solvent.

610
01:15:07,800 --> 01:15:19,800
So, think of the $10 stock example from
earlier, when the stock was going up from
$10 to $12 to $20 to $40.

611
01:15:19,800 --> 01:15:24,800
And it was always on 10 times earnings.

612
01:15:24,800 --> 01:15:39,800
When we walked into the market, your
friend was telling you that the stock was
expensive at $10 because other stocks in
the sector had a PE ratio of 5 and 6.

613
01:15:39,800 --> 01:15:42,800
So, it traded on a big premium to the
sector.

614
01:15:42,800 --> 01:15:43,800
So, it traded on a big premium to the
sector.

615
01:15:43,800 --> 01:15:48,800
Therefore, it must be, open quotes,
expensive, close quotes.

616
01:15:48,800 --> 01:15:50,800
But guess what?

617
01:15:50,800 --> 01:15:54,800
Every day in the market, somebody was
buying that stock at $10.

618
01:15:54,800 --> 01:15:57,800
And of course, people were selling it as
well.

619
01:15:57,800 --> 01:16:00,800
Otherwise, the people buying it couldn't
buy stock.

620
01:16:00,800 --> 01:16:07,800
There's liquidity and there's buyers at
$10 and sellers at $10.

621
01:16:07,800 --> 01:16:12,800
But the point is, the price is a rating.

622
01:16:12,800 --> 01:16:18,800
And the rating, the PE ratio, is a signal
of the quality of the company.

623
01:16:18,800 --> 01:16:21,800
And buyers are prepared to pay a premium.

624
01:16:22,800 --> 01:16:31,800
That $10 stock at that moment in time, in
January, at the beginning of the year, is
like the $500 lingerie.

625
01:16:31,800 --> 01:16:34,800
And your friend tells you, you should go
and short it.

626
01:16:34,800 --> 01:16:37,800
So, you go into the market and you short
it.

627
01:16:37,800 --> 01:16:39,800
Well, guess what happens?

628
01:16:39,800 --> 01:16:41,800
Stock goes to $12.

629
01:16:41,800 --> 01:16:43,800
Stock goes to $20.

630
01:16:43,800 --> 01:16:44,800
Stock goes to $40.

631
01:16:44,800 --> 01:16:49,800
And it's always, open quotes, expensive,
close quotes.

632
01:16:49,800 --> 01:16:53,800
And he's still telling you it's expensive
when it's $40.

633
01:16:54,800 --> 01:17:02,800
So, when you walked into the market at $10
and you and your friend decided

634
01:17:02,800 --> 01:17:08,800
that this stock must be a short because
it's trading on 10 times earnings.

635
01:17:08,800 --> 01:17:15,800
Do you think the market doesn't know that
the stock is trading on a premium to the
sector?

636
01:17:16,800 --> 01:17:17,800
Of course they do.

637
01:17:17,800 --> 01:17:22,800
There's nothing you know that the market
doesn't already know.

638
01:17:22,800 --> 01:17:27,800
They know it's on 10 times earnings and
they are prepared to pay for it.

639
01:17:27,800 --> 01:17:29,800
They're not stupid.

640
01:17:29,800 --> 01:17:37,800
The market is full of very smart people
and there's nothing you know that the
market doesn't already know.

641
01:17:37,800 --> 01:17:39,800
So, understand that.

642
01:17:40,800 --> 01:17:45,800
Secondly, the market can stay irrational
longer than you can stay solvent.

643
01:17:45,800 --> 01:17:52,800
So, you can see a stock trading on a big
premium to the rest of the sector.

644
01:17:52,800 --> 01:17:54,800
Everyone knows the premium.

645
01:17:54,800 --> 01:17:56,800
Everyone in the market knows the multiple.

646
01:17:56,800 --> 01:17:57,800
Okay?

647
01:17:57,800 --> 01:18:01,800
And you go in and you fight the market.

648
01:18:01,800 --> 01:18:04,800
You say, I think the market is wrong.

649
01:18:04,800 --> 01:18:07,800
Everyone in the market is incorrect.

650
01:18:07,800 --> 01:18:17,800
Me, little Jimmy sitting at home in my
home office in my mum's basement knows
more than the market.

651
01:18:17,800 --> 01:18:22,800
I know this stock is expensive, so I'm
going to short it.

652
01:18:22,800 --> 01:18:24,800
Well, guess what?

653
01:18:24,800 --> 01:18:32,800
Even if it's irrational, even if you think
it's irrational, the market can stay
irrational longer than you can stay
solvent.

654
01:18:32,800 --> 01:18:40,800
The stock can keep going up on the same
multiple and it can be totally justified
why it keeps going up.

655
01:18:40,800 --> 01:18:45,800
And you just lose all your money because
you argued with the market.

656
01:18:45,800 --> 01:18:50,800
And this happens to retail traders all the
time.

657
01:18:51,800 --> 01:18:59,800
We see it all the time when retail traders
come to ITPM and tell us that they've lost
money elsewhere.

658
01:19:00,800 --> 01:19:16,800
And they were essentially trying to take
on the market by shorting so-called
expensive stocks or even buying so-called
cheap stocks and using a lot of leverage
to do so.

659
01:19:16,800 --> 01:19:19,800
And they just get completely run over.

660
01:19:19,800 --> 01:19:22,800
To be fair, it's mostly not their fault.

661
01:19:22,800 --> 01:19:31,800
They've just been taught totally
incorrectly about what PE ratio actually
is and how to understand stock valuation.

662
01:19:31,800 --> 01:19:35,800
But don't worry, you're here to learn this
stuff.

663
01:19:35,800 --> 01:19:47,800
And like I said earlier, when you learn
this once and you understand it properly,
you only have to learn it once and process
it and make it stick.

664
01:19:47,800 --> 01:20:08,800
Make the concept stick, then you have to
put it into practice over and over again
and keep practicing valuing companies and
understanding relative valuations of
stocks and how to interpret them properly.

665
01:20:09,800 --> 01:20:15,800
Now, we're going to go through a real
example here from the market.

666
01:20:16,800 --> 01:20:25,800
We're going to be looking at Wingstop, a
stock called Wingstop, and everyone knows
McDonald's.

667
01:20:25,800 --> 01:20:39,800
So what you're looking at on the screen
here is a snapshot of the food services
and drinking places subsector from
accommodation and food services.

668
01:20:39,800 --> 01:20:48,800
So the main NAICS sector is the
accommodation and food services sector.

669
01:20:48,800 --> 01:20:52,800
And the subsector name is food services
and drinking places.

670
01:20:52,800 --> 01:20:58,800
And this screenshot is taken in the first
quarter of 2021.

671
01:20:58,800 --> 01:21:05,800
And we're going through a process here of
identifying outliers.

672
01:21:05,800 --> 01:21:11,800
So we look, first of all, for high PE
stocks and low PE stocks.

673
01:21:11,800 --> 01:21:16,800
And we start to make an interpretation of
the landscape of this sector.

674
01:21:16,800 --> 01:21:25,800
So something else we've included as well,
we've obviously included market cap as
well.

675
01:21:25,800 --> 01:21:28,800
So you can see market cap there in the
columns.

676
01:21:28,800 --> 01:21:34,800
And we've got the first year, one year
forward price earnings ratio.

677
01:21:34,800 --> 01:21:43,800
We've got two years forward PE ratio, and
we've got three years forward PE ratio.

678
01:21:43,800 --> 01:21:50,800
But we haven't included EPS here for 2021.

679
01:21:50,800 --> 01:21:55,800
We're just doing this for simplicity to
show the example of how it works first.

680
01:21:55,800 --> 01:22:08,800
So for Wingstop, we can see Wingstop, the
ticker WING, is on 100 times 2021
earnings.

681
01:22:08,800 --> 01:22:15,800
And McDonald's MCD is on 25 times 2021
earnings.

682
01:22:15,800 --> 01:22:23,800
And we can run a simple backtest of what
things looked like a year ago as an
example.

683
01:22:23,800 --> 01:22:32,800
So in the previous year, in 2020, to see
what the multiples were and how the stock
prices performed.

684
01:22:32,800 --> 01:22:40,800
So similar to our $10 example of stocks
that stay on the same multiple when they
go up

685
01:22:40,800 --> 01:22:43,800
and stay on the same multiple when they go
down.

686
01:22:43,800 --> 01:22:48,800
Obviously, it's a bit more messy in the
real world, but that's the concept.

687
01:22:48,800 --> 01:22:55,800
And I want to show you a real example of
this so you understand it and the concept
actually sticks.

688
01:22:55,800 --> 01:22:58,800
So let's run a little backtest.

689
01:22:58,800 --> 01:23:01,800
Let's run a little role play.

690
01:23:01,800 --> 01:23:08,800
There's a chart on the screen here of the
S&P 500 in 2020.

691
01:23:08,800 --> 01:23:23,800
And we can see the lows put in on March
23rd, 2020 in the S&P 500 during the COVID
sell-off.

692
01:23:23,800 --> 01:23:30,800
So imagine for a moment that you're
sitting at your desk on March 23rd, 2020.

693
01:23:30,800 --> 01:23:33,800
The whole world is melting down.

694
01:23:33,800 --> 01:23:35,800
The whole world's burning around you.

695
01:23:35,800 --> 01:23:38,800
And we're at the COVID lows.

696
01:23:38,800 --> 01:23:40,800
Obviously, you don't know that at the
time.

697
01:23:40,800 --> 01:23:47,800
So everything's gone a little bit crazy,
but you're a well-trained trader and you
understand valuation.

698
01:23:47,800 --> 01:23:53,800
And you're thinking, it's time to put some
money to work.

699
01:23:53,800 --> 01:23:55,800
What do you go and buy?

700
01:23:55,800 --> 01:24:05,800
Well, you obviously at the time on March
23rd at the COVID lows, because everything
was getting hammered.

701
01:24:05,800 --> 01:24:12,800
You would have been faced with a multitude
of choices of stocks to buy across the
market.

702
01:24:12,800 --> 01:24:21,800
But just imagine at the time you were
looking in the food services and drinking
places sector for stocks to buy.

703
01:24:21,800 --> 01:24:22,800
What do you buy?

704
01:24:22,800 --> 01:24:32,800
Well, you will have been presented with a
very similar table to the one that we took
a snapshot of in Q1 2021.

705
01:24:33,800 --> 01:24:37,800
There will have been lots of high PE
stocks and low PE stocks.

706
01:24:37,800 --> 01:24:49,800
And at the time, obviously, near the COVID
lows, going into them and out of them, EPS
estimates were being revised down
aggressively across the board.

707
01:24:49,800 --> 01:25:02,800
And both Wingstop and McDonald's had
positive EPS estimates on March 23rd, 2020
at the S&P 500 lows.

708
01:25:02,800 --> 01:25:17,800
So, if we look at the table below and we
look at the stock price, for example, of
Wing, on the 23rd of March 2020, Wing's
stock price was $62.76.

709
01:25:17,800 --> 01:25:32,800
On that day, research analysts in the
market had a consensus estimate for 2020
EPS for Wing of 83 cents earnings.

710
01:25:32,800 --> 01:25:51,800
Forward looking, one year forward EPS
estimate for 2020, which would give Wing a
price earnings ratio on that day, the 23rd
of March of 75.61.

711
01:25:51,800 --> 01:26:08,800
So, we take $62.76, the stock price on the
day, and divide that by 0.83, so the 83
cents, and we get a PE ratio of 75.61.

712
01:26:09,800 --> 01:26:20,800
Now, if we look at McDonald's, the stock
price on the 23rd of March 2020, was
$137.10.

713
01:26:20,800 --> 01:26:37,800
The EPS estimate, the consensus forward
looking estimate for EPS for 2020 for
McDonald's from research analysts, was
$8.20 on the 23rd of March.

714
01:26:37,800 --> 01:26:49,800
Which, on the 23rd of March, would mean
that McDonald's had a PE ratio of 16.72.

715
01:26:49,800 --> 01:26:59,800
Now, we've added another column in there,
which is the stock prices of the two
stocks, Wing and McDonald's, on the 15th
of June.

716
01:26:59,800 --> 01:27:10,800
Wing on the 15th of June was $120.14, and
McDonald's was $189.49.

717
01:27:10,800 --> 01:27:19,800
That's what their stock prices were on
that day, on the 15th of June 2020.

718
01:27:19,800 --> 01:27:22,800
And the reason we put those in there will
become apparent very soon.

719
01:27:22,800 --> 01:27:29,800
Now, we've also calculated something at
the bottom of the screen there, which is
called the PE differential.

720
01:27:29,800 --> 01:27:43,800
And we've taken that from our previous
table for Q1 2021, where Wing had a PE
ratio of 99.6, and McDonald's of 25.11.

721
01:27:43,800 --> 01:28:04,800
And to work out the differential, we
simply divide the Wing PE ratio of 99.6 by
the McDonald's PE ratio of 25.11, which
gives us a factor of 3.96.

722
01:28:04,800 --> 01:28:19,800
Now, that, if we multiply it by 100, gives
us a 396% premium that Wing is trading on
a PE premium versus McDonald's.

723
01:28:19,800 --> 01:28:23,800
So, that is in Q1 2021.

724
01:28:23,800 --> 01:28:35,800
Wing is trading in Q1 2021 on a 396%
differential PE premium to McDonald's.

725
01:28:35,800 --> 01:28:41,800
So, as we go through this example, just
try to remember all of the data.

726
01:28:41,800 --> 01:28:53,800
But just recall in the back of your mind,
the stock prices of Wing and McDonald's on
June 15, 2020.

727
01:28:53,800 --> 01:29:04,800
And the PE differential of Wing versus
McDonald's, the 396% in Q1 2021.

728
01:29:04,800 --> 01:29:18,800
Now, we can see our stock price and EPS
estimates from the market, the consensus
estimates, together for 2020 on a chart
here.

729
01:29:18,800 --> 01:29:21,800
So, first of all, we're looking at Wing
stop.

730
01:29:21,800 --> 01:29:28,800
So, we're looking at the whole of 2020
here, and also the first part of 2021.

731
01:29:28,800 --> 01:29:41,800
And we're looking at the estimate periods
for the EPS estimate for annual estimates
of 2020.

732
01:29:41,800 --> 01:29:56,800
So, the reason why it goes up to February
2021 is because that's when they reported
their 2020 final EPS number for the year.

733
01:29:56,800 --> 01:29:59,800
So, at that moment, it can never change.

734
01:29:59,800 --> 01:30:20,800
But what you can see on the screen here is
how on the red line, the EPS estimates
from research analysts changed over the
year 2020 versus the white line, which is
the stock price changing every day in the
year 2020.

735
01:30:20,800 --> 01:30:25,800
So, we can have a look at our March COVID
lows.

736
01:30:25,800 --> 01:30:37,800
And as we know, the Wing stock price on
March 23rd was $62.76.

737
01:30:37,800 --> 01:30:43,800
So, we have our stock price on the
left-hand side of the chart.

738
01:30:43,800 --> 01:30:46,800
So, the scale is on the left-hand side.

739
01:30:46,800 --> 01:30:56,800
And on March 23rd, we also had our EPS
estimate coming in at $0.83 for the year
2020.

740
01:30:56,800 --> 01:31:04,800
Our scale for EPS is on the right-hand
side of the chart.

741
01:31:04,800 --> 01:31:11,800
And then you can see what happens to both
the stock price and EPS estimates.

742
01:31:11,800 --> 01:31:12,800
What happens?

743
01:31:12,800 --> 01:31:24,800
Well, the stock price in the last week of
March goes up and continues going up all
of April and then sideways from April to
June.

744
01:31:24,800 --> 01:31:30,800
And the stock is around $120 by June 15th.

745
01:31:30,800 --> 01:31:33,800
So, we know that from our table.

746
01:31:33,800 --> 01:31:43,800
And the EPS estimates started to move up
in April and then went up a lot in May.

747
01:31:43,800 --> 01:31:48,800
So, you can see the move in the red line
going up a lot in May.

748
01:31:48,800 --> 01:31:54,800
And this was our EPS estimate moving up.

749
01:31:54,800 --> 01:31:58,800
So, the consensus EPS estimates moving up.

750
01:32:00,800 --> 01:32:03,800
And it moved up very aggressively.

751
01:32:03,800 --> 01:32:14,800
And by June, the EPS estimate for Wing was
$1.04.

752
01:32:14,800 --> 01:32:22,800
And by June 15th, we know the stock price
from our table for Wing was $120.14.

753
01:32:23,800 --> 01:32:29,800
And the EPS estimate, which we're now
adding into our analysis, was $1.04.

754
01:32:30,800 --> 01:32:33,800
And what about if we look at McDonald's?

755
01:32:33,800 --> 01:32:38,800
We can do exactly the same analysis as we
just did with Wing.

756
01:32:38,800 --> 01:32:44,800
We can look at the stock price and EPS on
the same chart for 2020.

757
01:32:45,800 --> 01:32:51,800
And we have our scale for the stock price
on the left-hand side of the chart.

758
01:32:52,800 --> 01:32:54,800
And our stock price is in white.

759
01:32:54,800 --> 01:33:01,800
And we have our EPS estimate, our research
analyst consensus estimate, which is
always changing.

760
01:33:03,800 --> 01:33:04,800
We have that in red.

761
01:33:04,800 --> 01:33:10,800
And our scale for the 2020 EPS estimate is
on the right-hand side of the chart.

762
01:33:11,800 --> 01:33:14,800
And we can do the same analysis as we did
with Wing.

763
01:33:15,800 --> 01:33:23,800
We can look at the stock price and the EPS
estimate from the market at the Covid lows
on March 23rd.

764
01:33:24,800 --> 01:33:30,800
And we can look at it three months later
on June 15th and just see what happened to
both of them.

